  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data view findings:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` has 14,074 requests from 1,001 distinct client IPs (`clientip`). Status codes are 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441). The top destination countries are CN, IN and US. The timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so part of the data is in the future relative to today (2026-10-11).\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-46e81178 Web logs overview\" (id `eff-46e81178-web-logs-overview-2ad0be6db619`) has five panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Requests over time (area, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n- Requests by HTTP response code (pie, on `response.keyword`)\n- 5xx responses (metric, filtered to `response.keyword` = 503)\n- Top countries by requests (bar, on `geo.dest`)\n\nI set the dashboard time range to 2026-10-01 through 2026-12-31 so it shows all the sample data. The default of the last 7 days would show nothing.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-46e81178 5xx spike\" (id `b7d4f9e3-61f2-44f6-b827-6857476e5941`) is an Elasticsearch query rule:\n- Runs every 1m over a 5m window on `@timestamp`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- The query matches `response.keyword` with the wildcard `5??`, so it covers every 5xx code, not only 503.\n- Consumer `stackAlerts`, enabled. The rule status is `ok`, and alerting health reports `ok`.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- I used the Kibana MCP tools rather than the raw API. `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` isn't set in my shell, so I didn't call the REST API directly.\n- I created the rule disabled and then enabled it in a separate step.\n- The \"5xx spike\" alert has no action connector, so it records alerts but sends no notifications. I can add one if you want notifications.",
